// Testing/HarnessSelfTest.maxon — the gate over the SPEC HARNESS'S OWN REFUSALS, and over the one
// selection rule no spec case can reach.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A REFUSAL NOTHING RUNS IS A CLAIM, NOT A DOOR** — this project's own rule, and until this
// file the harness broke it in six places at once. `SpecParser` refuses an empty ```RequiredRuntime
// block, an empty section pin, and a pin or runtime block on a case that never links; `TargetPrinter`
// refuses a ```RequiredRuntime name nothing emits and one the fragment already shows; and
// `pinsAnyResult` refuses a case with a program and nothing to check. Every one of them was written,
// reviewed, and then run by NOTHING. A refusal that has never fired is a refusal nobody has seen work.
//
// ⚠ **AN ORDINARY SPEC CASE STRUCTURALLY CANNOT PIN THESE, WHICH IS WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AND IS
// SHAPED THE WAY IT IS.** The three `SpecParser` refusals are `panic`s raised while the harness parses
// its OWN corpus, in the parent process, before a worker is spawned (`buildPlans`). A deliberately
// malformed spec committed to `specs-shv2/` would therefore abort the WHOLE SUITE rather than fail one
// case — the gate would take the suite down with it every single run.
//
// ⚠ **AND PINNING THE PANIC TEXT WOULD NOT DO EITHER.** A Maxon panic prints `panic at
// <file>:<line>: <message>`, so an expectation carrying the whole text embeds `SpecParser.maxon:807`
// and goes red on every edit that moves a line above it — a false-red generator wired to an unrelated
// property. What this file compares is a SUBSTRING of the message, chosen to name the refusal and to
// contain no file, no line and no stack frame.
//
// ⇒ **THE SHAPE IS THE RUNNER'S OWN, ONE LEVEL UP.** `compileStagedTest` already proves a compiler
// diagnostic by SPAWNING the compiler and reading its exit code and stderr; this proves a HARNESS
// refusal by spawning the HARNESS, pointed at a fixture corpus of deliberately-malformed specs, and
// reading the same two things. Each fixture is its own directory holding one `.md`, because
// `buildPlans` parses every spec in a directory before any filter is applied — so a second malformed
// spec beside the first would never be reached, and the case that never ran would look exactly like
// the case that passed.
//
// ⚠ **THE CORPUS IS DELIBERATELY NOT UNDER `specs-shv2/`.** `specFilePaths` lists `*.md` directly
// under the spec directory, so a subdirectory there would be invisible TODAY — but "invisible because
// the walk does not recurse" is a property of the walk, and the whole point of these files is that
// they detonate if anything ever reads them as ordinary specs. Living under `maxon-shv2/Testing/`,
// they are not in the corpus at all, whatever the walk grows into.
//
// ⭐ **AND ONE THING HERE IS NOT A REFUSAL AT ALL** — `requireNetworkGateHolds`, which proves the
// live-network gate still keeps `<!-- network: live -->` cases out of a default run, still lets
// `--network` back in, and still SAYS which cases it dropped. It is in this file because it is the
// same shape and the same instrument (spawn this harness at a fixture corpus, read exit code and
// output), and it is in a SECOND corpus root because its fixture must be accepted rather than
// refused — see `GatesRelativeDir`.

// Where the fixture corpus lives, relative to the repo root — anchored at the working directory for
// the reason `LadderIndex` and `spec-test`'s own default spec directory are: the suite is invoked from
// the repo root, and a run from anywhere else has already failed to find `specs-shv2`.
// It is ALSO the recursion guard's one source: `isFixtureCaseDir` reads this path's last segment rather
// than a second constant naming the same directory.
let FixturesRelativeDir = "maxon-shv2/Testing/harness-fixtures"

// ⭐ **THE SECOND CORPUS ROOT — the harness's BEHAVIOUR gates, not its refusals**, and it is a second
// root rather than another case directory under the first for one reason: `requireHarnessRefusalsFire`
// sweeps EVERY subdirectory of its root and requires each to be a deliberately-malformed spec that the
// harness REFUSES. This corpus's fixture is the opposite — a well-formed spec that must be ACCEPTED,
// parsed in full, and then partly left out of the run. Filed under one root, each sweep would read the
// other's cases as its own kind and report a defect that is not there.
//
// It is a corpus root all the same, which is the part that matters to `isHarnessFixtureSubRun`: its case
// directories are driven by SPAWNING this harness at them, so a sub-run must recognise itself here
// exactly as it does there — see `harnessCorpusRoots`.
let GatesRelativeDir = "maxon-shv2/Testing/harness-gates"

// The FIRST gate case: `<gates root>/<this>/network-gate.md`, holding a single case marked
// `<!-- network: live -->`. The directory name and the spec's `<spec>/<test>` label are both named here
// because the assertions below are ABOUT that exact case, and a gate that silently ran some other
// directory would be a gate that proves nothing.
let NetworkGateCaseDir = "network"
let NetworkGateSpecName = "network-gate"
let NetworkGateTestName = "network-gate.live"

// ⭐ **THE SECOND GATE CASE — the orphaned-golden census.** `<gates root>/<this>/orphan-gate.md`, with a
// `fragments/` tree this gate PLANTS and then removes. Named here for `NetworkGateCaseDir`'s reason: the
// assertions below are about these exact files, and a gate that silently ran some other directory would
// prove nothing.
let OrphanGateCaseDir = "orphan-golden"
let OrphanGateSpecName = "orphan-gate"

// ⭐⭐ **THE LANE THE ORPHANS ARE PLANTED IN IS FIXED, AND DELIBERATELY NOT THE HOST'S.** The census
// covers EVERY `fragments/<target>` directory rather than the run's own target — that is the property
// orphan debris on an unrun lane depends on — so planting into a lane the sub-run does not build for is
// what proves it. On a Windows host it happens to coincide; everywhere else the gate is asserting the
// cross-lane reach directly.
let OrphanGateLane = "x64-windows"

// The three planted files, one per census arm. Each is named for what makes it uncomparable, because
// that name is what a reader of a failed gate reads first.
let OrphanGateMissingCase = "a-case-this-spec-no-longer-declares"
let OrphanGateRestrictedCase = "orphan-gate.restricted-elsewhere"
let OrphanGateVanishedSpec = "a-spec-that-no-longer-exists"
let OrphanGateVanishedCase = "a-case-of-a-spec-that-is-gone"

// What a planted golden holds. Its CONTENT is never read — the census asks only whether anything
// compares the file — so it says what it is, for whoever finds one left behind by an interrupted run.
let OrphanGatePlantedContent = "// planted by HarnessSelfTest.requireOrphanedGoldensAreReported and removed by it; never compared\n"

let FixtureSpecExtension = ".md"

// The line a fixture carries to say which refusal it is about: `<!-- expect-refusal: <substring> -->`.
//
// ⭐ **IT LIVES IN THE FIXTURE, NOT IN A ROSTER HERE, AND THAT IS THE `LadderIndex` LESSON APPLIED.**
// A hand-maintained table of (case, expected text) beside a directory of cases is one fact written
// twice, and the half that drifts is always the table. The case SET is derived by listing the
// directory and the expectation is read out of the case itself, so adding a refusal to the harness is
// one new directory and nothing else.
//
// It sits ABOVE the fixture's `## Tests` heading, so `extractTests` never scans the line — the marker
// grammar it shares (`<!-- key: value -->`) is read by `markerValue`, the same reader every other
// marker in a spec goes through.
let ExpectRefusalMarker = "<!-- expect-refusal:"

// ⭐ **PROVE EVERY HARNESS REFUSAL STILL FIRES.** Called ONCE, by the spec suite's parent process,
// before a worker is spawned — the placement `requireLadderIndexComplete` and `requireRunnerAvailable`
// already use, and for their reason: this is a property of the TREE, not of any one test, so there is
// no test to fail and nothing downstream that could act on it. It PANICS, naming the fixture and what
// its sub-run actually did.
//
// `harnessExe` is what a fixture sub-run IS — this harness, which since G20 is also the compiler each
// fixture's cases are built with. ⛔ **IT USED TO FORWARD `--compiler=` AS WELL**, so that the two
// PRINTER refusals were asked of the compiler under test rather than of whatever compiler this harness
// happened to be. That flag is gone with the in-process compile (an in-process compile has no second
// binary to name), and the distinction it protected went with it: one binary is both halves.
//
// ⚠ The sub-runs deliberately do NOT inherit the run's `--target`. What a fixture asks is whether the
// HARNESS refuses a malformed directive, which no target changes — and two of the fixtures reach a
// refusal in the IR printer, which a target emitting no Target IR (wasm32) never runs at all. Under a
// cross-target suite run, forwarding the target would turn those two into false reds about nothing.
export function requireHarnessRefusalsFire(harnessExe FilePath, specDir FilePath)
	if isHarnessFixtureSubRun(specDir) 'thisIsAFixtureSubRun'
		return
	end 'thisIsAFixtureSubRun'

	let root = try FilePath.from(FixturesRelativeDir) otherwise panic("HarnessSelfTest: '{FixturesRelativeDir}' is not a valid path")
	let cases = try Directory.list(root) otherwise panic("HarnessSelfTest: could not list {FixturesRelativeDir} — it holds the deliberately-malformed specs that prove the harness's own refusals still fire. Run the suite from the repo root.")

	// EVERY fixture is run and every failure is reported TOGETHER, rather than stopping at the first.
	// One edit routinely breaks several — `refuseVacuousDirective` is reached through three fences — and
	// a report naming one of them sends the reader back for another full sweep per remaining fixture.
	// (`LadderIndex` reports its three directions in one message for the same reason.)
	var checked = 0 as FixtureTally
	var broken = StringArray.create()
	for caseDir in cases 'eachCase'
		if not Directory.isDirectory(caseDir) 'notACase'
			continue
		end 'notACase'

		match checkFixture(harnessExe, caseDir: caseDir) 'verdict'
			fired then break 'verdict'
			didNotFire(reason) then broken.push(reason)
		end 'verdict'
		checked = checked + 1
	end 'eachCase'

	// An EMPTY corpus would let every refusal go unproven while this function reported nothing at all —
	// the exact silence it exists to remove, one level up. `LadderIndex` refuses a missing directory for
	// the identical reason.
	if checked == 0 'noFixtures'
		panic("HarnessSelfTest: {FixturesRelativeDir} holds no case directories, so not one harness refusal was proven to fire. Each refusal is a subdirectory containing one {FixtureSpecExtension} that trips it.")
	end 'noFixtures'

	if broken.isEmpty() 'everyRefusalFired'
		return
	end 'everyRefusalFired'

	// ⚠ `panic` takes a STRING LITERAL — a `let`-bound String is rejected outright — so the list is
	// built as text and INTERPOLATED. Same constraint `LadderIndex.requireLadderIndexComplete` documents.
	let report = indentedList(broken, indent: BrokenHarnessFixtureIndent)
	panic("HarnessSelfTest: {broken.count()} of {checked} harness refusals did not fire on the fixture written to trip them. A refusal nothing runs is a claim, not a door.{report}")
end 'requireHarnessRefusalsFire'

// How deep each failing fixture sits under the sentence that introduces it — one paragraph per line.
let BrokenHarnessFixtureIndent = "\n  "

// One fixture's verdict. A union rather than an empty-String reason, so "the refusal fired" carries no
// vestigial text and there is no sentinel to re-derive.
union FixtureVerdict
	fired
	didNotFire(reason String)
end 'FixtureVerdict'

// How many fixture cases one sweep proved. Named because it is the count the empty-corpus refusal
// reads, not a loop counter.
typealias FixtureTally = int(0 to u64.max)

// ⭐ **IS THIS PROCESS A FIXTURE SUB-RUN?** Asked by TWO callers with quite different stakes, which is
// why it is exported rather than folded into the guard above: `requireHarnessRefusalsFire` asks it to
// avoid an unbounded 8-ary fan-out, and `Main.runSpecTest` asks it because a sub-run must NOT take the
// tree lock its own parent is already holding — the one nested case in this driver, and the ⛔ that
// would otherwise turn a lock into a refusal against itself. One question, one answer; two spellings
// of "am I a fixture" is precisely the drift this function's own warning below is about.
export function isHarnessFixtureSubRun(specDir FilePath) returns bool
	let roots = harnessCorpusRoots()
	for root in roots 'eachRoot'
		if isFixtureCaseDir(specDir, root: root) 'underThisRoot'
			return true
		end 'underThisRoot'
	end 'eachRoot'

	return false
end 'isHarnessFixtureSubRun'

// EVERY corpus under `Testing/` whose case directories are driven by SPAWNING this harness at them —
// the refusal fixtures and the behaviour gates.
//
// ⭐ **ONE LIST, BECAUSE "AM I A SUB-RUN?" HAS ONE ANSWER.** A second corpus that the recognition
// question did not know about is not a smaller version of that question going wrong: its sub-runs would
// each fail to recognise themselves, re-run the WHOLE self-test (an unbounded process fan-out — see
// `isFixtureCaseDir` for the measurement) and then be turned away by the tree lock their own parent is
// holding. Adding a corpus is adding a line here, and nothing else.
function harnessCorpusRoots() returns FilePathArray
	var roots = FilePathArray.create()
	roots.push(try FilePath.from(FixturesRelativeDir) otherwise panic("HarnessSelfTest: '{FixturesRelativeDir}' is not a valid path"))
	roots.push(try FilePath.from(GatesRelativeDir) otherwise panic("HarnessSelfTest: '{GatesRelativeDir}' is not a valid path"))
	return roots
end 'harnessCorpusRoots'

// Is `specDir` one of the fixture case directories — i.e. is this process a fixture sub-run? A fixture
// case is driven by spawning this harness at `<root>/<case>`, so the question is whether `specDir`'s
// PARENT is the corpus root. Derived from the path the sub-run was GIVEN rather than signalled by a
// private flag, because a flag is a second way of saying which directory this is and the directory
// already says it. A path with no parent (the filesystem root) is not one, which is the only thing the
// failure arm can honestly say.
//
// ⚠ **THE NAME IT COMPARES AGAINST COMES FROM `root`, NOT FROM A SECOND SPELLING OF IT.** This guard
// held a `let FixturesDirName = "harness-fixtures"` beside `FixturesRelativeDir`'s
// `".../harness-fixtures"` — the same directory written down twice, with nothing making the two agree.
// Rename the corpus and update only the constant that names the PATH (the obvious edit, and the only
// one anything reads for the listing) and this guard silently stops matching: every sub-run then fails
// to recognise itself and re-runs the whole self-test, whose own sub-runs each do the same. That is an
// unbounded fan-out on every `spec-test` invocation — one branch per fixture directory plus the gate's two
// runs, so it widens every time a fixture is added — and no compile error anywhere.
// (Measured before the fix: pointing a run at a case directory whose parent was spelled differently
// took 0.575s against the guarded 0.061s — the self-test running inside what is structurally a
// sub-run.)
//
// ⚠ **A TRAILING SEPARATOR IS STRIPPED FIRST, AND SINCE THE TREE LOCK THAT MATTERS.** `a/b/c/` and
// `a/b/c` name one directory, but the empty last component IS a component: `parent()` answers `a/b/c`
// for the first and `a/b` for the second (measured), so the unstripped spelling asks the question one
// level too shallow and every fixture dir stops recognising itself. Shell tab completion produces that
// spelling by default. The miss used to cost only the fan-out below; now it costs a REFUSAL — an
// unrecognised sub-run takes the tree lock and is turned away by the parent that spawned it
// (measured: `spec-test <fixture>/` exited 2 saying the checkout was BUSY, while the same path without
// the slash ran the fixture). A path that is nothing BUT separators has no case name and is not one.
function isFixtureCaseDir(specDir FilePath, root FilePath) returns bool
	var caseDir = specDir
	while caseDir.filename().isEmpty() 'trailingSeparator'
		caseDir = try caseDir.parent() otherwise return false
	end 'trailingSeparator'

	let parent = try caseDir.parent() otherwise return false
	return parent.filename() == root.filename()
end 'isFixtureCaseDir'

// Run ONE fixture and require that the harness refused it, saying what the fixture says it will say.
//
// TWO conditions, and both are necessary. A NON-ZERO exit alone proves only that something went wrong
// — a fixture whose program failed to compile for an unrelated reason would satisfy it — so the
// message must also carry the refusal's own words. And the substring alone would not do either: it
// could appear in output from a run that nevertheless reported PASS.
//
// A fixture that could not be RUN AT ALL (a spawn failure, a missing or unmarked spec) panics on the
// spot instead of joining the report: that is the harness's instrument being broken rather than a
// refusal being gone, and the two must not read alike.
function checkFixture(harnessExe FilePath, caseDir FilePath) returns FixtureVerdict
	let caseName = caseDir.filename()
	let expected = expectedRefusalIn(caseDir, caseName: caseName)

	let run = try runProcess(Executable.path(harnessExe), argv: fixtureArgv(caseDir), workingDirectory: inheritedWorkingDir()) otherwise (e) 'spawnFailed'
		panic("HarnessSelfTest: could not run the '{caseName}' fixture ({e.displayReason()}) — it proves a harness refusal still fires, and a refusal nothing runs is a claim")
	end 'spawnFailed'

	// BOTH streams: a `SpecParser` refusal panics the sub-run's parent onto stderr, while the two
	// printer refusals reach the reader as a FAILED TEST's reason on stdout. One rule reads either.
	let said = everythingSaid(run)

	if run.exitCode == 0 'harnessAcceptedIt'
		return FixtureVerdict.didNotFire("'{caseName}' was ACCEPTED (exit 0). It is a deliberately-malformed spec, and the refusal that should have caught it — the one that says '{expected}' — did not fire. Either that refusal was removed, or this fixture no longer trips it.")
	end 'harnessAcceptedIt'

	if not said.contains(expected) 'wrongRefusal'
		return FixtureVerdict.didNotFire("'{caseName}' failed (exit {run.exitCode}) but for the WRONG REASON. Expected the output to contain '{expected}', and it said:\n{said}")
	end 'wrongRefusal'

	return FixtureVerdict.fired
end 'checkFixture'

// The command line EVERY sub-run of a fixture corpus starts from: this harness, pointed at ONE case
// directory.
//
// Shared by the refusal sweep and the network gate below so the two cannot drift into asking different
// questions of the same harness.
function fixtureArgv(caseDir FilePath) returns StringArray
	var argv = StringArray.create()
	argv.push(SpecTestCommandName)
	argv.push(caseDir.toString())
	return argv
end 'fixtureArgv'

// EVERYTHING a sub-run said, both streams joined. One rule, because which stream carries the evidence
// is not something a caller should have to know: a parser refusal panics onto stderr, a failed test's
// reason lands on stdout, and a trailer note is on stderr again.
function everythingSaid(run ProcessCapture) returns String
	return "{run.stdout}\n{run.stderr}"
end 'everythingSaid'

// ⭐⭐ **PROVE THE LIVE-NETWORK GATE STILL GATES.** The cases across `tcp-client`, `async-tcp` and
// `http-client` that open a socket to a REAL EXTERNAL HOST are marked `<!-- network: live -->`, left out
// of a default run and selected by `--network`. Three things have to hold, and each is a separate way for
// the mechanism to be quietly gone:
//
//   1. a marked case is NOT SELECTED by a default run — the marker is read at all;
//   2. it IS selected, compiled and run under `--network` — the flag reaches BOTH halves of the pool;
//   3. the exclusion is REPORTED — the excluded cases do not simply vanish out of the count.
//
// ⚠ **(3) IS THE ONE THAT WOULD ROT SILENTLY.** (1) and (2) are visible the moment somebody runs the
// suite either way. A census that stopped printing looks exactly like a suite with nothing to say, and
// the cases it stopped naming look exactly like cases that were never written.
//
// ⛔ **AND (1) IS WHY THIS CANNOT BE AN ORDINARY SPEC CASE.** What is under test is which cases the
// harness SELECTS, and a spec case is something the harness has already selected. So the shape is
// `requireHarnessRefusalsFire`'s: spawn this harness at a fixture corpus and read what it did.
//
// Called ONCE by the suite's parent, beside the other tree-level guards, and PANICS naming every
// property that failed — all of them together, because one careless edit breaks several.
export function requireNetworkGateHolds(harnessExe FilePath, specDir FilePath)
	if isHarnessFixtureSubRun(specDir) 'thisIsAFixtureSubRun'
		return
	end 'thisIsAFixtureSubRun'

	let root = try FilePath.from(GatesRelativeDir) otherwise panic("HarnessSelfTest: '{GatesRelativeDir}' is not a valid path")
	// Interpolated rather than passed as the bare constant: a top-level `let` String is ambiguous between
	// `join`'s String and FilePath overloads (the same constraint `Main.resolveSpecDir` documents).
	let caseDir = root.join("{NetworkGateCaseDir}")
	if not Directory.isDirectory(caseDir) 'noFixture'
		panic("HarnessSelfTest: the live-network gate fixture is not at {GatesRelativeDir}/{NetworkGateCaseDir}, so nothing proves that a `{NetworkMarker} {NetworkLiveValue} -->` case is kept out of a default run — or that `{NetworkFlag}` brings it back. Run the suite from the repo root.")
	end 'noFixture'

	let label = "{NetworkGateSpecName}/{NetworkGateTestName}"

	// What a spawn failure would have to say, spelled ONCE for this gate's two runs. A local rather
	// than a module constant because it interpolates `NetworkFlag`, and a top-level `let` may not.
	let proves = "a marked case stays out of a default run and comes back under `{NetworkFlag}`"

	// ⭐ WHAT "THE CASE RAN" LOOKS LIKE, BUILT FROM THE REPORT'S OWN RENDERER rather than from a second
	// spelling of the verdict token. An empty reason makes this a PREFIX of the real line, which is what
	// `contains` needs; the reason itself is the fixture's deliberately-wrong stdout and is not the point.
	// ⚠ It is a verdict line and NOT merely the label, because the label ALSO appears in the exclusion
	// census — an assertion on the label alone would be satisfied by the very run it is meant to exclude.
	let ranVerdict = verdictLine(TestOutcome.fail(""), label: label)

	// The census entry for this case, from the one function that renders a trailer entry. Same argument:
	// what is asserted is the harness's own output, so a change to how a note is laid out reaches this
	// gate instead of stranding it.
	let censusEntry = resultNoteEntry(NetworkGateSpecName, testName: NetworkGateTestName, detail: "")

	var broken = StringArray.create()

	let byDefault = everythingSaid(runGateFixture(harnessExe, caseDir: caseDir, includeNetwork: false, proves: proves))
	if byDefault.contains(ranVerdict) 'defaultRanIt'
		broken.push("a DEFAULT run of the fixture RAN '{label}', which is marked `{NetworkMarker} {NetworkLiveValue} -->`. Every suite in this tree would then be a coin toss on a third party's uptime.\n")
	end 'defaultRanIt'

	if not byDefault.contains(censusEntry) 'defaultSaidNothing'
		broken.push("a DEFAULT run left '{label}' out and did not NAME it. An excluded case nobody counts is indistinguishable from one that was never written, and the run said:\n{byDefault}\n")
	end 'defaultSaidNothing'

	if not byDefault.contains(NetworkMarker) 'defaultGaveNoReason'
		broken.push("a DEFAULT run named the case it left out but not the REASON: the census headline no longer mentions `{NetworkMarker}`, so a reader is told coverage is missing and not what would bring it back.\n")
	end 'defaultGaveNoReason'

	let withNetwork = everythingSaid(runGateFixture(harnessExe, caseDir: caseDir, includeNetwork: true, proves: proves))
	if not withNetwork.contains(ranVerdict) 'flagDidNotIncludeIt'
		broken.push("a `{NetworkFlag}` run did NOT run '{label}'. The flag no longer reaches selection, so every `{NetworkMarker} {NetworkLiveValue} -->` case is unreachable by any command line — shelved in all but name. It said:\n{withNetwork}\n")
	end 'flagDidNotIncludeIt'

	if withNetwork.contains(censusEntry) 'flagStillExcludedIt'
		broken.push("a `{NetworkFlag}` run reported '{label}' as EXCLUDED. The gate and the census disagree about what the flag means, and the report says a case was skipped that the same run went on to execute.\n")
	end 'flagStillExcludedIt'

	if broken.isEmpty() 'everyPropertyHeld'
		return
	end 'everyPropertyHeld'

	// ⚠ `panic` takes a STRING LITERAL — see `requireHarnessRefusalsFire` for the constraint this shape
	// comes from.
	let report = indentedList(broken, indent: BrokenHarnessFixtureIndent)
	panic("HarnessSelfTest: the live-network gate FAILED {broken.count()} of its checks. It is what keeps a suite's verdict from depending on tcpbin.com and httpbin.org being up, and what keeps the cases it drops from vanishing silently.{report}")
end 'requireNetworkGateHolds'

// ⭐ **ONE RUN OF ANY GATE FIXTURE — shared by both gates, because they differ only in a flag and in
// which gate a failure names.** A second copy is how the two would come to spawn the harness
// differently, which is the drift `fixtureArgv` already exists to prevent one level down.
//
// A spawn failure PANICS on the spot rather than joining the caller's report, for `checkFixture`'s
// reason: that is the instrument being broken rather than the property being gone, and the two must not
// read alike. `proves` is what the caller would otherwise have written into its own panic — the sentence
// that tells a reader what stopped being checked.
function runGateFixture(harnessExe FilePath, caseDir FilePath, includeNetwork bool, proves String) returns ProcessCapture
	var argv = fixtureArgv(caseDir)
	if includeNetwork 'wantsLiveNetwork'
		argv.push(NetworkFlag)
	end 'wantsLiveNetwork'

	return try runProcess(Executable.path(harnessExe), argv: argv, workingDirectory: inheritedWorkingDir()) otherwise (e) 'spawnFailed'
		panic("HarnessSelfTest: could not run a gate fixture ({e.displayReason()}) — it proves {proves}, and a gate nothing runs is a claim")
	end 'spawnFailed'
end 'runGateFixture'

// ⭐⭐ **PROVE THE SUITE STILL NAMES A GOLDEN NOTHING COMPARES.** Three reports ask what a golden did —
// did it DRIFT, was it COMPARED, is it TRACKED — and none of them can see a `.test` whose case no longer
// exists: `checkTestFragment` opens the file BY PATH and never enumerates, and the census loops over
// RESULTS, so a file with no result appears in none of its columns. `GoldenTracking
// .reportUncomparedGoldens` is the fourth question, and this is what keeps it answered.
//
// ⛔ **AN ORDINARY SPEC CASE STRUCTURALLY CANNOT PIN THIS, for `requireHarnessRefusalsFire`'s reason one
// level down.** What is under test is a report the PARENT prints after every worker has finished, about
// the whole spec directory — a case cannot observe it, and committing an orphan to `specs-shv2/` to be
// observed would put a permanently-uncomparable file in the corpus the census exists to keep clean.
//
// ⭐ **SO THE ORPHANS ARE PLANTED AND REMOVED, not committed** — and that buys the half a committed
// fixture could not have: a NEGATIVE CONTROL. The gate runs the fixture twice, once with the files there
// and once without, and requires the report to appear and then to disappear. A census that named
// everything would satisfy the first run and fail the second; one that named nothing fails the first.
// (Planted under the gate corpus's own gitignored `fragments/`, so an interrupted run leaves nothing
// tracked behind, and the next run overwrites it.)
//
// Called ONCE by the suite's parent beside the other tree-level guards, and PANICS naming every property
// that failed.
export function requireOrphanedGoldensAreReported(harnessExe FilePath, specDir FilePath)
	if isHarnessFixtureSubRun(specDir) 'thisIsAFixtureSubRun'
		return
	end 'thisIsAFixtureSubRun'

	let root = try FilePath.from(GatesRelativeDir) otherwise panic("HarnessSelfTest: '{GatesRelativeDir}' is not a valid path")
	// Interpolated rather than passed as the bare constant, for `requireNetworkGateHolds`' reason.
	let caseDir = root.join("{OrphanGateCaseDir}")
	if not Directory.isDirectory(caseDir) 'noFixture'
		panic("HarnessSelfTest: the orphaned-golden gate fixture is not at {GatesRelativeDir}/{OrphanGateCaseDir}, so nothing proves that a `.test` no case reads is still NAMED by a suite run. Run the suite from the repo root.")
	end 'noFixture'

	// Spelled once for this gate's two runs, beside the network gate's own.
	let proves = "a golden nothing compares is still NAMED by a suite run"

	let planted = plantOrphanGoldens(caseDir)
	let withOrphans = everythingSaid(runGateFixture(harnessExe, caseDir: caseDir, includeNetwork: false, proves: proves))
	removePlantedGoldens(planted)
	let withoutOrphans = everythingSaid(runGateFixture(harnessExe, caseDir: caseDir, includeNetwork: false, proves: proves))

	var broken = StringArray.create()

	// The expectations are BUILT BY THE REPORT'S OWN RENDERER (`uncomparedEntry`), never spelled here —
	// `requireNetworkGateHolds`' argument, and the reason `UncomparedReason` is a union rather than a
	// sentence built at each site. A change to how an entry reads reaches this gate instead of stranding it.
	for expected in expectedOrphanEntries() 'eachExpected'
		if not withOrphans.contains(expected) 'notNamed'
			broken.push("a run over the fixture did NOT name a planted golden. Expected its report to contain:\n{expected}and it said:\n{withOrphans}\n")
		end 'notNamed'
	end 'eachExpected'

	// THE NEGATIVE CONTROL. Without it the gate is satisfied by a census that reports every golden in the
	// tree, which is the same as no census at all — and it is the half that would rot silently, because a
	// report that fires too often looks exactly like a report that works.
	if withoutOrphans.contains(UncomparedHeadline) 'reportedACleanTree'
		broken.push("the SAME fixture, with the planted goldens removed, still printed the census. Then the report does not depend on there being anything to report, and every suite run in this tree is telling its reader about files that are fine. It said:\n{withoutOrphans}\n")
	end 'reportedACleanTree'

	if broken.isEmpty() 'everyPropertyHeld'
		return
	end 'everyPropertyHeld'

	// ⚠ `panic` takes a STRING LITERAL — see `requireHarnessRefusalsFire` for the constraint.
	let report = indentedList(broken, indent: BrokenHarnessFixtureIndent)
	panic("HarnessSelfTest: the orphaned-golden census FAILED {broken.count()} of its checks. It is the only thing in this tree that can see a golden nothing compares — a reference file that cannot be wrong, because nothing reads it.{report}")
end 'requireOrphanedGoldensAreReported'

// The three entries a planted run must print — one per census arm, each built by the census's own
// renderer so that this gate cannot come to disagree with the report about what an entry says.
function expectedOrphanEntries() returns StringArray
	var expected = StringArray.create()
	expected.push(uncomparedEntry("{OrphanGateLane}/{OrphanGateSpecName}/{OrphanGateMissingCase}{FragmentExtension}", reason: UncomparedReason.noSuchCase(OrphanGateSpecName, caseName: OrphanGateMissingCase)))
	expected.push(uncomparedEntry("{OrphanGateLane}/{OrphanGateSpecName}/{OrphanGateRestrictedCase}{FragmentExtension}", reason: UncomparedReason.laneCannotSelectIt(OrphanGateSpecName, caseName: OrphanGateRestrictedCase, laneName: OrphanGateLane)))
	expected.push(uncomparedEntry("{OrphanGateLane}/{OrphanGateVanishedSpec}/{OrphanGateVanishedCase}{FragmentExtension}", reason: UncomparedReason.noSuchSpec(OrphanGateVanishedSpec)))
	return expected
end 'expectedOrphanEntries'

// Write the three goldens nothing can ever compare, and hand back what was written so it can be removed
// again. Every failure PANICS on the spot rather than joining the report, for `checkFixture`'s reason:
// an instrument that could not be set up is not a property being gone, and the two must not read alike.
function plantOrphanGoldens(caseDir FilePath) returns FilePathArray
	let laneDir = caseDir.join("{FragmentsSubdir}").join("{OrphanGateLane}")

	var planted = FilePathArray.create()
	planted.push(plantOneGolden(laneDir.join("{OrphanGateSpecName}"), caseName: OrphanGateMissingCase))
	planted.push(plantOneGolden(laneDir.join("{OrphanGateSpecName}"), caseName: OrphanGateRestrictedCase))
	planted.push(plantOneGolden(laneDir.join("{OrphanGateVanishedSpec}"), caseName: OrphanGateVanishedCase))
	return planted
end 'plantOrphanGoldens'

// One planted golden, with its per-spec directory made if it is not there. `Directory.create` is mkdir -p.
function plantOneGolden(specGoldenDir FilePath, caseName String) returns FilePath
	if not Directory.create(specGoldenDir) 'noDirectory'
		panic("HarnessSelfTest: could not create the orphaned-golden gate's fixture directory. The gate plants a `.test` nothing declares and requires the run to name it; without the directory it would prove nothing and would say so as a missing entry.")
	end 'noDirectory'

	let path = specGoldenDir.join("{caseName}{FragmentExtension}")
	try File.writeText(path, content: OrphanGatePlantedContent) otherwise panic("HarnessSelfTest: could not plant the orphaned-golden gate's fixture file. A gate that cannot set up its own subject proves nothing.")
	return path
end 'plantOneGolden'

// Take the planted goldens away again, so the tree this gate ran in is the tree it found. A removal that
// fails PANICS: a leftover planted file is a permanent, deliberate orphan in a corpus whose whole point
// is that there are none, and the next run's negative control would read it as a live defect.
function removePlantedGoldens(planted FilePathArray)
	for path in planted 'eachPlanted'
		try File.delete(path) otherwise panic("HarnessSelfTest: could not remove a golden the orphaned-golden gate planted. It would stay behind as a real orphan, and the next run's negative control would report the gate's own litter as a defect.")
	end 'eachPlanted'
end 'removePlantedGoldens'

// The substring `caseDir`'s one `.md` says its refusal must print — see `ExpectRefusalMarker`.
//
// Every failure here is the FIXTURE being wrong rather than the harness, and each is a panic for the
// reason the whole file is: a fixture that cannot say what it expects proves nothing, and a self-test
// that skipped it would be the silence this file removes.
function expectedRefusalIn(caseDir FilePath, caseName String) returns String
	let text = try File.readText(fixtureSpecIn(caseDir, caseName: caseName)) otherwise panic("HarnessSelfTest: could not read the '{caseName}' fixture's spec file")

	for line in text.split("\n") 'eachLine'
		if line.contains(ExpectRefusalMarker) 'isTheMarker'
			let expected = markerValue(line)
			if expected.isEmpty() 'noValue'
				panic("HarnessSelfTest: the '{caseName}' fixture's '{ExpectRefusalMarker}' marker names no text. It must carry the part of the refusal's message that identifies it — and NOT the `<file>:<line>` a panic prints in front of it, which moves on every edit above it.")
			end 'noValue'
			return expected
		end 'isTheMarker'
	end 'eachLine'

	panic("HarnessSelfTest: the '{caseName}' fixture carries no '{ExpectRefusalMarker} … -->' line, so nothing says WHICH refusal it is about — and a fixture that merely fails proves only that something went wrong.")
end 'expectedRefusalIn'

// The one `.md` in a fixture case directory. EXACTLY one, because a case is one refusal: a second spec
// beside it would never be reached (`buildPlans` parses in listing order and the first refusal panics),
// so it would sit there looking like coverage it is not.
function fixtureSpecIn(caseDir FilePath, caseName String) returns FilePath
	let entries = try Directory.list(caseDir) otherwise panic("HarnessSelfTest: could not list the '{caseName}' fixture directory")

	var specs = FilePathArray.create()
	for entry in entries 'eachEntry'
		if entry.fileExtension() == FixtureSpecExtension 'isSpec'
			specs.push(entry)
		end 'isSpec'
	end 'eachEntry'

	if specs.count() != 1 'notExactlyOne'
		panic("HarnessSelfTest: the '{caseName}' fixture holds {specs.count()} '{FixtureSpecExtension}' files, and a case is ONE refusal. A second spec beside the first is never reached — the first one's refusal aborts the sub-run — so it would look like coverage it is not.")
	end 'notExactlyOne'

	return try specs.get(0) otherwise panic("HarnessSelfTest: the '{caseName}' fixture's spec list is empty despite its count being 1")
end 'fixtureSpecIn'
